# `⋮` is the typing tower as glyph (pun + spec)

## The pun

`⋮` (U+22EE VERTICAL ELLIPSIS) — three vertical dots — depicts
the typing tower as notation.  In `Fridge ⋮`, the dots ARE the
rungs of the upward stream: Fridge stacked above Fridge stacked
above Fridge, the productive codata visualised compactly.  The
glyph names exactly what the Tower abstraction computes.

It's also a *spec*.  The right semantic reading of `c ⋮` isn't
"shorthand for `c : c`" — it's:

> Build the tower at this name+path; use its rung-1 as the type
> annotation.

That's precisely `kindOf` applied to `TyConV c declPath Z`,
which under self-stratification gives `TyConV c declPath (S Z)`.
The shorthand and the explicit form coincide because of the
fixpoint at `predLv (LVar p) = LVar p`: every rung at the
parametric level looks the same, so `c : c` and `c ⋮` produce
the same elaboration result, but the *reason* is the tower's
stable tail, not a textual substitution.

## What it picks out

The stable upward stream of `meetTowers`' termination criterion
maps onto the three dots:

```
Fridge       <-- value (ctor at predLv parent)
  :          <-- one rung up via kindOf
Fridge       <-- type at parent's level
  ⋮          <-- the stable codata-tail above, all at the same
               parametric LVar via the fixpoint
```

The `:` is one deck transformation.  The `⋮` is the entire
stable tail above — codata as glyph.  Each character in the
notation has algebraic content.

## Why no elaborator change is needed

The parser desugars `c ⋮` to `tyConRef ann c lhsPath` directly
— same AST node the body-mutual prescan + tcBinders extension
would produce for an explicit `c : c`.  Once `predLv (LVar p) =
LVar p` gave the level coordinate its fixpoint (for the
`data Weird : Weird` story), and the body-mutual prescan added
sibling names to `tcBinders` (for `Iso : Iso { One : One; … }`),
and HypLinf's parent-fallback let unbound tyConRefs resolve at
the parent's level — the elaboration of `c ⋮`'s desugaring fell
out for free.  The shorthand is the surface payoff of three
prior commits' algebraic work.

## Future extension — parametric `X a ⋮`

Ambiguous between `X a : X` (bare identifier) and `X a : X a`
(applied to own params).  Different semantics:

- Bare: X is at kind X, a separate beast from `X a`.  The
  shorthand drops the parameter from the kind side.
- Applied: X a's kind is X a — fully self-applied
  stratification, including the parameter.

Today's grammar is nullary-only for `⋮`-style decls; the
singleton use case (Iso, Swap, Mirror) doesn't need parameters.
When parametric data wants `⋮`, the design choice picks itself
based on which reading lands cleanly: `X a` carries the
parameter into the kind tower naturally if `kindOf` on `App`
already unfolds via the head's kind (it does — `kindOf env
(TyAppV f _) = kindOf env (hRun f)`), so the "applied"
reading is consistent with the existing machinery.  Lean
toward that when it becomes load-bearing.

## Recorded as pun for posterity

The user's "It's a typing (tower) pun" framing crystallised the
syntax-as-spec point.  The three-character glyph names the
algebraic invariant; the parser implements the desugaring; the
elaborator wasn't touched because the abstraction is already
load-bearing in the layer below.  This is the cleanest test of
"did we build the right substrate" we've had — the substrate
admits the notation that names it.
